    "Here is a Python function that abstracts states into groups using the provided grid world:\n\n```python\ndef state_abstraction(grid_world):\n    \"\"\"\n    Abstracts states in the given grid world into groups.\n    \n    Args:\n        grid_world (dict): A dictionary representing the grid world with 'grid', 'start', and 'goal' keys.\n\n    Returns:\n        list[list[int]]: A 2D list where each sublist contains indices of abstracted states.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Initialize a dictionary to store visited cells\n    visited = {(i, j): False for i in range(len(grid_world['grid'])) for j in range(len(grid_world['grid'][0]))}\n    \n    # Function to perform DFS from a given cell\n    def dfs(i, j):\n        if 0 <= i < len(grid_world['grid']) and 0 <= j < len(grid_world['grid'][0]) and not visited[(i, j)]:\n            visited[(i, j)] = True\n            group.append(grid_world['grid'][i][j])\n            if grid_world['grid'][i][j] != 'X':\n                dfs(i - 1, j)  # Up\n                dfs(i + 1, j)  # Down\n                dfs(i, j - 1)  # Left\n                dfs(i, j + 1)  # Right\n    \n    abstracted_states = []\n    \n    # Perform DFS for each unvisited cell and store the result in a group\n    for i in range(len(grid_world['grid'])):\n        for j in range(len(grid_world['grid'][0])):\n            if not visited[(i, j)]:\n                group = []\n                dfs(i, j)\n                abstracted_states.append(group)\n    \n    return abstracted_states\n\n# Example usage:\ngrid_world = {\n    \"goal\": 16,\n    \"grid\": [[0, 2, 3, 5, 7], [1, \"X\", 4, \"X\", \"X\"], [\"X\", 9, 6, 10, 12], [17, \"X\", 8, \"X\", 14], [15, 13, 11, \"X\", 16]],\n    \"start\": 0\n}\n\nprint(state_abstraction(grid_world))\n```\nThis function performs a depth-first search (DFS) on the grid world and groups connected states together. It treats 'X' cells as obstacles that disconnect adjacent states. The result is a list of lists where each sublist contains indices of 